{
  "case_id": "2011-10-699",
  "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, Mr. Lewin, what power is Congress exercising here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0000-0010",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument first this morning in Case 10-699, Zivotofsky v. Clinton."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Congress is supreme, then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What -- so, you're suggesting that the outcome of this of case would be different, from your perspective, if Congress said you have to say \"Jerusalem, Israel.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0000-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What -- what were we scrutinizing in the Steel Seizure case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0000-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Are you suggesting -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But a personal identification choice can also have significant foreign policy implications, can it not? Is -- is that an either-or situation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Court's view about Congress's power with regard to (a) passports -- and again, I go back to the fact that in Zemel v. Rusk, in Haig v. Agee, in Kent v. Dulles, in all these passport cases, this Court said we look to see whether what the President does is authorized by Congress, whether implicitly or otherwise. So that, I submit, that with regard to passports, you need the congressional authority, whether it's implicit or express."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "that you're skipping over the question that the D.C. Circuit decided. I take it your view is it's not a political question; so, the Court should resolve the merits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Our view is it's not a political question because it is like many other questions that affect foreign policy. And the Court said in Baker v. Carr, not every decision that touches on foreign affairs or foreign policy is a political question that can't be determined."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The executive has determined that the passports it issues should not identify Israel as the place of birth for persons born in Jerusalem. Petitioner seeks relief under section 214(d) that would countermand that executive judgment. But under the Constitution, that is an exercise of the executive's exclusive recognition power. The Constitution commits that power exclusively to the executive, and neither a court nor the Congress can override that judgment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What are then the reasons that -- because your friend says that this is an a fortiori case from everything, because all of these words -- every time the word \"exclusive power\" has appeared in any source -- I think that's what you're saying -- it is meant that the President can act without supporting authority from Congress."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You say this is exclusively committed to the President, and, therefore, it is a non-justiciable political question. How is that different from saying it's our job to decide cases, it is justiciable, and then you can argue that the answer of that analysis is that it's exclusively committed to the President? I don't understand why labeling it a political question advances the analysis much."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, doesn't it depend on what the question is? In order to decide whether it's a political question, you have to identify the question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The exercise of the recognition power depends, we think, on three things that make it clear that it needs to be exclusive. The first is timing; the second is expertise; and the third is a need for secrecy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: So, if that provision were not in the Constitution, would you be making the same argument you are now?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2011-10-699",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "23b8a204b8836873f7e4a1f94a1661b5b4eca0831e9c1819020e8c1cf7943f55",
      "evidence_id": "ev-23b8a204b883-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: The case is submitted."
    },
